<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/advanced/population-genetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Population genetics puts <strong>evolution into numbers</strong>. Darwin's theory said
    descent with modification; the genetics of the 1900s made it computable: track the
    frequencies of alleles across generations, and identify the forces that move them —
    natural selection, genetic drift, mutation, migration. The field's master stroke is
    having a <strong>null model</strong>: Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium (1908) describes how
    frequencies behave when nothing acts on them. With "no evolution" defined exactly,
    evolution becomes measurable — any deviation from the null names its cause and size.
    That is the pattern the whole subject follows, and it is why population genetics
    became the mathematical spine of the <strong>modern synthesis</strong> (1930s–40s),
    connecting Mendel's individuals (the inheritance lesson) to Darwin's populations and
    species (the evolution and speciation lessons).
  </p>
  <p>
    The objects are populations, not individuals: an <strong>allele frequency</strong> is
    the fraction of all gene copies carrying one variant, and a <strong>genotype
    frequency</strong> is the fraction of individuals with each allele combination. One
    individual's genotype is a coin toss; a population's frequencies are the coin's
    statistics — the same statistics the probability-thinking of the entire science
    relies on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four forces, anchored on the null model" widget="equationStep" config={popgenCfg} />

  <h2>Hardy–Weinberg: the null model, used daily</h2>
  <p>
    With two alleles A and a at frequencies p and q (p + q = 1), random mating makes
    zygotes a Punnett square at population scale: <strong>p² AA : 2pq Aa : q² aa</strong> —
    and, the remarkable part, these frequencies return <em>unchanged</em> every
    generation, whatever the starting mix. After one generation of random mating the
    population is permanently in equilibrium. Each equilibrium condition encodes one
    evolutionary force, so the model is really a checklist: large population (else
    drift), no selection, no mutation, no migration, random mating (else inbreeding
    shifts genotype frequencies while leaving allele frequencies alone — a subtle point
    examiners love). Its most-used application is the recessive-disease carrier
    calculation:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the everyday forensic use of Hardy–Weinberg — from disease frequency to hidden carriers" widget="equationStep" config={hwCfg} />

  <p>
    The same arithmetic runs genetic counselling (phenylketonuria: q² = 1/10,000 gives
    q = 0.01 and carriers 2pq ≈ 1 in 50), carrier screening programmes, and forensic
    profile probabilities (the molecular-biology lesson's 20-locus product assumes
    Hardy–Weinberg within populations, with corrections applied for structure).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Hardy–Weinberg "assumes no evolution, so it describes
    real populations badly." Backwards — that is the point. It is a <em>reference</em>:
    measure a population's genotype counts, compare with p² : 2pq : q², and the excess
    or deficit of heterozygotes tells you which force is acting (heterozygote excess →
    recent hybridisation or overdominance; deficit → inbreeding or the Wahlund effect
    of mixing structured populations). A null model is an instrument, not a description.
  </p>

  <h2>Selection: fitness and its three shapes</h2>
  <p>
    Give each genotype a <strong>relative fitness</strong> w (expected reproductive
    contribution, fittest set to 1) and allele frequencies move predictably — Δq is
    computable from the fitnesses, generation by generation. Three geometries matter.
    <strong>Directional</strong> selection (industrial melanism: peppered moths' dark form
    rising as soot blackened the trunks, and falling again as air cleaned) pushes an
    allele toward fixation. <strong>Stabilising</strong> selection (human birth weight:
    both extremes raise infant mortality — the classic 1950s data) removes extremes and
    preserves the mean; it maintains variation rather than consuming it.
    <strong>Disruptive</strong> selection favours both tails over the middle (Darwin's
    finch bill-size bimodality on Santa Cruz island) and can split a population — the
    bridge to the speciation lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Heterozygote advantage</strong> is the canonical balancing case. Sickle-cell:
    the homozygote (aa) suffers severe disease (fitness ≈ 0.25 historically), the
    homozygote AA is malaria-vulnerable (fitness ≈ 0.9 in endemic regions), the carrier
    Aa resists malaria best (fitness 1). Equilibrium settles where the two homozygote
    disadvantages balance: q = s/(s + t) = 0.1/(0.1 + 0.75) ≈ 0.12 — matching observed
    allele frequencies near 0.1–0.15 in West African populations. The disease is the
    price the population pays for malaria resistance; move malarial populations to
    America (no malaria) and selection drains the allele away — geography, through
    selection, writes the disease map. <strong>Mutation–selection balance</strong>
    explains the rest of rare genetic disease: selection purges deleterious alleles,
    mutation recreates them, equilibrium sits near q ≈ √(μ/s) for recessives — with
    mutation rate μ ≈ 10⁻⁵ and a lethal allele (s = 1), q ≈ 3 × 10⁻³, roughly the
    observed frequency of many recessive disorders. Rare diseases are the standing wave
    between creation and destruction.
  </p>

  <h2>Drift: sampling noise with real teeth</h2>
  <p>
    Reproduction is sampling: the next generation's alleles are drawn from the current
    pool with randomness, and in a small population the sampling error is large.
    <strong>Genetic drift</strong> wobbles frequencies randomly; it has no direction, is
    strongest in small populations, and its two signatures are loss of variation and
    divergence between populations. Quantitatively, heterozygosity decays as
    H<sub>t</sub> = H₀(1 − 1/(2Nₑ))ᵗ: an effective population of 50 loses about 18% of
    its heterozygosity in 20 generations (0.99²⁰ ≈ 0.82) — which is why conservation
    biologists treat Nₑ, not census size, as the number that matters (only breeders
    count; unequal sex ratios and variance in family size shrink Nₑ below the head
    count, sometimes drastically). Three classic case studies: <strong>founder
    effects</strong> (island colonists carrying a skewed sample — the Amish's elevated
    Ellis–van Creveld syndrome from a few founders; Pingelap achromatopsia from a
    typhoon's ~20 survivors); <strong>bottlenecks</strong> (northern elephant seals
    hunted to ~20–30 animals in the 1890s, now 30,000-plus yet almost genetically
    uniform; cheetahs' near-identical immune genes, so skin grafts between individuals
    are accepted); and <strong>fixation</strong> — drift eventually fixes one allele,
    and the probability an allele ultimately fixes equals its current frequency, which
    is the neutral theory's cornerstone: most molecular evolution is drift, not
    selection (Kimura, 1968).
  </p>

  <h2>Gene flow: the force that connects</h2>
  <p>
    Migration mixes populations and homogenises them: with a fraction m of each
    population replaced by migrants each generation, differences decay geometrically —
    one migrant per generation between populations is enough, in theory, to prevent
    divergence by drift alone. The tension between flow and the other forces structures
    everything: gene flow versus selection produces <strong>clines</strong> (allele
    frequencies gradient — the mapped latitude gradients in human skin-pigmentation
    genes); gene flow versus drift produces <strong>isolation by distance</strong>
    (nearby populations similar, distant ones diverged). The standard yardstick is
    <strong>F<sub>ST</sub></strong>, the fraction of total genetic variance held between
    populations: near 0 means well-mixed, near 1 means isolated. Human continental
    F<sub>ST</sub> ≈ 0.15 — most human genetic variation is <em>within</em> any
    population, a result with direct bearing on how race and genetics intersect. And
    gene flow crosses species lines too: the ~2% Neanderthal DNA in non-African genomes
    (the speciation lesson's webbed tree) is introgression — flow, caught across a
    species boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>Inbreeding, small populations and the conservation arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Random mating is the assumption; its violation has its own algebra. The
    <strong>inbreeding coefficient</strong> F measures the excess homozygosity from
    mating among relatives, and it climbs by 1/(2Nₑ) per generation in a closed
    population — inbreeding exposes recessive lethals (each individual carries, on
    average, a handful masked) and erodes fitness: the well-documented inbreeding
    depression of captive-breeding programmes, where litter sizes shrink and juvenile
    mortality climbs with pedigree depth. Conservation practice manages F explicitly:
    studbooks pair distant lineages, and <strong>genetic rescue</strong> can reverse the
    spiral — the Florida panther, down to ~25 cats and failing reproductively by the
    1990s, recovered after introducing eight Texas females in 1995, their hybrid
    descendants showing both restored fitness and the hoped-for heterozygosity. The
    quantitative targets come straight from the drift section: keep Nₑ near 50+ to
    slow inbreeding depression (the short-term rule), near 500+ to keep adaptive
    variation against drift (the long-term rule), and remember Nₑ is typically a
    fraction of the census count. When a reserve's budget cannot buy that many
    animals, corridor design — reconnecting populations so gene flow does the mixing —
    is population genetics applied to a map.
  </p>

  <h2>Where adaptation comes from: new mutations or standing variation?</h2>
  <p>
    A subtlety with real consequences: when the environment shifts, does the population
    wait for the right new mutation (slow — waiting time ~1/μ per locus, millennia for
    a specific beneficial letter) or recombine variants already segregating
    (<strong>standing variation</strong>, fast — the alleles are present, selection
    just re-weights them)? Modern examples suggest standing variation usually wins:
    insecticide resistance alleles predate the insecticides in many documented cases,
    and the melanism of peppered moths was a recolouring of existing variation. This
    reframes conservation urgency: a population's capacity to adapt to tomorrow's
    climate is largely the <em>current</em> genetic variation it holds — which is why
    the variation-losing forces of this page (bottlenecks, inbreeding) are treated as
    debts with future interest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Human deep history shows the whole toolkit in one dataset. Altitude adaptation in
    Tibet rests on an EPAS1 haplotype that genomic archaeology traced to
    <strong>Denisovan introgression</strong> — adaptation sourced from another archaic
    human species; skin-pigmentation gradients are polygenic, built from variants at
    half a dozen loci across continents; and the lactase-persistence sweep (Going
    deeper) shows a cultural innovation (dairying) rewriting the gene pool within
    historical time. Genes, geography and culture form one feedback system, and
    population genetics is its measurement science.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Selection's fingerprints on the genome.</strong> A strongly favoured allele
    drags its neighbours along (<strong>genetic hitchhiking</strong>), leaving a
    <strong>selective sweep</strong>: a long haplotype block of reduced variation around
    the selected site, decaying with recombination time. The clearest human example is
    lactase persistence: the −13910*T variant (one letter, upstream of the lactase gene)
    swept to near-fixation in northern European dairying populations within ~7,500 years,
    and East African pastoralists carry <em>different</em> variants with the same effect —
    convergent evolution, visible as parallel sweeps. Distinguishing sweeps from
    demographic history is the field's hardest inference problem: a bottleneck mimics a
    sweep, which is why papers argue over the human "recent acceleration" of selection.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantitative genetics: when everything matters a little.</strong> Height,
    blood pressure, crop yield — traits shaped by thousands of loci plus environment.
    The <strong>breeder's equation</strong> R = h²S (response = heritability times
    selection differential) is population genetics aggregated over loci, and it runs
    agriculture (the yield curves of the agriculture pages) and animal breeding alike.
    Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) now find the individual loci: height is
    ~80% heritable yet its known variants explain only a fraction — the "missing
    heritability" debate, where the leading suspect is many loci of small effect plus
    rare variants, an open empirical question with clinical consequences for polygenic
    scores.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The coalescent and deep history.</strong> Run the genealogies backward —
    the speciation lesson introduces this — and the same equations date human
    migrations (out of Africa ~60,000 years, bottlenecked founder chains readable in
    today's genomes), Neanderthal admixture timing, and the effective sizes of
    long-dead populations. The modern synthesis is being extended, not overthrown: the
    open questions are whether development biases which mutations matter
    ("constructive neutral evolution" and evolvability), how much adaptation was
    polygenic in recent human history, and how to predict — from a genome — which
    populations can survive the climate and disease pressures coming next. That last
    question is the conservation connection: minimum viable population sizes
    (roughly Nₑ above 100 to dodge inbreeding depression short-term, above 1,000 to
    retain adaptive potential — the famous 50/500 rule's modern descendants) are
    population genetics converted directly into land-use policy.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hardy (1908) &amp; Weinberg (1908); Kimura, <em>The Neutral Theory of Molecular Evolution</em>; Gillespie, <em>Population Genetics: A Concise Guide</em>; Itan et al. (2009) on lactase persistence; the Modern Synthesis literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
